{{/* Config template — chezmoi init generates ~/.config/chezmoi/chezmoi.toml from this.
     The profile is resolved ONCE per machine (persisted in the generated config);
     `chezmoi apply` never depends on ambient env vars after that.

     Profiles (invasivity levels):
       personal  — your own machine: identity, GUI tools, full dev toolchains
       shared    — shared account (ags isolated shells, lab PCs): minimal, NO git identity
       robot     — robots/appliances: host CLI tools, no identity, no GUI, no toolchains
       container — devcontainers/DevPod: minimal; identity kept (DevPod injects git creds)
       kinisi    — kinisi_ros dev containers: container, but with $HOME trees bind-mounted
                   from the host and ~/.pixi owned by the image, so chezmoi must stay off
                   both. Selected only by container/bootstrap.sh, never prompted.

     Non-interactive selection: CHEZMOI_PROFILE=robot chezmoi init --apply <repo>
     Change an existing machine: CHEZMOI_PROFILE=<profile> chezmoi init --apply

     `toolbox` is the one capability flag that exists purely as a size knob, and
     it exists because the ownership flags below deliberately are not one (see
     .chezmoiignore.tmpl): a container needs the same *config* as the host, so
     "smaller" cannot be expressed by refusing to write paths. What a container
     does not need is the tool payload. Every devlaunch workspace was syncing
     ~25 pixi envs / ~1.27GB — go, isd, forgit, vim, zellij, a second copy of
     devlaunch — to run `claude` and `gh`, and paid for it on every create
     because a fresh container has no package cache. `toolbox` is off for the
     container classes and on everywhere else, so hosts are unchanged and a
     container installs only the floor listed in the pixi manifest.

     The fall-through is `shared`, deliberately, and it is the answer to "nothing
     here identifies this machine". It used to be `personal`, which is the worst
     available answer to that question: `personal` is the only profile with
     identity, gui, heavy AND host all on, so an unrecognised environment got the
     most invasive install in the matrix.

     Which default governs where, because the two paths differ: `promptChoiceOnce`
     does NOT quietly fall back without a tty -- it fails outright ("could not open
     a new TTY"), and yields this default only under `--promptDefaults`. So this
     default covers a hand-run interactive `chezmoi init`. Every scripted path goes
     through install.sh, which resolves a profile itself and passes it as
     `CHEZMOI_PROFILE=<profile> chezmoi init --apply --force`, bypassing the prompt
     entirely -- so install.sh's own fall-through is the one that actually fired,
     and it is fixed there too. Both now land on `shared`.

     What that cost, concretely: a kinisi dev container entered by any route other
     than the bootstrap gets no KINISI_INSTANCE handshake, so it never reaches the `kinisi`
     row. It fell through to `personal`, and because the compose files bind-mount
     ~/.config, ~/.cache and ~/.local/share from the host, "the container's
     ~/.config" and "the host's ~/.config" are one directory -- so it wrote a
     kitty.conf rendered for /home/kinisi onto the host, and put 79 /home/kinisi
     keys in the host's shared chezmoi state DB.

     `shared` closes the capability half of that: identity, gui, heavy and host all
     go off, so no git identity is written, and the GUI and toolchain trees
     (.config/kitty, .config/nvim) are not touched. It does NOT close the ownership
     half -- pixi, xdg, gitconfig and claudecfg are all still on for `shared`, so on
     these flags alone an unidentified container may still write host-mounted trees.

     That half is closed in install.sh, not here, and it has to be: this template
     generates ~/.config/chezmoi/chezmoi.toml, so in a container whose ~/.config is
     the host's, the file carrying these flags is itself one of the casualties -- no
     value set below can protect it. install.sh therefore refuses to run at all when
     $HOME's config trees are on a different filesystem from $HOME *and* findmnt
     names their source subpath under some other home. See "Refusing a home that is
     not this machine's" in the README. */}}
{{- $choices := list "personal" "shared" "robot" "container" "kinisi" -}}
{{- $profile := env "CHEZMOI_PROFILE" -}}
{{- if not $profile -}}
{{-   if env "AGS_SHELL" -}}
{{-     $profile = "shared" -}}
{{-   else if env "DEVPOD" -}}
{{-     $profile = "container" -}}
{{-   else -}}
{{-     $profile = promptChoiceOnce . "profile" "Machine profile" $choices "shared" -}}
{{-   end -}}
{{- end -}}
{{/* backward compat with pre-profile-matrix names */}}
{{- if eq $profile "full" }}{{ $profile = "personal" }}{{ end -}}
{{- if eq $profile "devpod" }}{{ $profile = "container" }}{{ end -}}
{{- if not (has $profile $choices) -}}
{{-   fail (printf "unknown profile %q (want one of %s)" $profile (join ", " $choices)) -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- $personal := eq $profile "personal" -}}
{{/* Container classes: DevPod/devcontainer workspaces and kinisi containers. Both have
     ~/.gitconfig supplied by the runtime (DevPod injects git credentials), so chezmoi
     must not write it. ~/.claude used to be listed here on the same grounds and it was
     never true of DevPod: devlaunch mounts nothing of ~/.claude and never did. It
     injects the credential as an environment variable instead, so the *config* in there
     -- settings.json, hooks, model, theme -- was simply missing from every workspace,
     which is why the status line came up blank. `claudecfg` below therefore reads an
     observed mount rather than this list. */}}
{{- $containerish := has $profile (list "container" "kinisi") -}}
{{/* Which of the two config-dir owners we are looking at, as observed by install.sh
     rather than guessed from the profile name: "foreign" (mounted in from another
     home), "local" (ours to write), or empty when nobody looked. See install.sh's
     has_foreign_mount_under. */}}
{{- $claudeMount := env "DOTFILES_CLAUDE_MOUNT" -}}

# File modes chezmoi writes are otherwise taken from the umask of whatever shell
# invoked it, which makes them a property of the terminal rather than of this
# repo. On a machine with the Ubuntu default of 0002 an apply writes 664/775; from
# a shell at 0022 it writes 644/755 -- so `chezmoi diff` reported mode-only drift
# on whichever files were last written by the other one, and `/sync` kept
# surfacing it. Neither side is wrong and git records none of it (it tracks only
# the executable bit), so it never converged: re-adding does not touch modes, and
# applying just moved the drift to the other set of files.
#
# Pinning it makes the mode a property of the config. 0022 over 0002 because these
# are one person's dotfiles: group-writable buys nothing, and the containers here
# share a uid with the host. `private_` files are unaffected either way -- chezmoi
# forces those to 600/700 regardless of umask.
umask = 0o022

[data]
    profile  = {{ $profile | quote }}
    # Capability matrix — the ONLY place profile names are interpreted.
    # Templates gate on these flags, never on profile names.
    identity  = {{ or $personal (eq $profile "container") }}  # git user name/email
    gui       = {{ $personal }}                               # nerd fonts, uhk-agent, nvtop
    heavy     = {{ $personal }}                               # rust, nodejs, devpod, ccache (nvim moved to .editor)
    host      = {{ or $personal (eq $profile "robot") }}      # git, openssh, curl, unzip
    monitor   = {{ not $containerish }}                       # htop, btop
    agents    = {{ or $personal (eq $profile "shared") }}     # codex, opencode CLI agents
    toolbox   = {{ not $containerish }}                       # full CLI/TUI toolbox; containers get only the claude/gh floor
    # `editor` is the second size knob, and it exists for the same reason `toolbox`
    # does — except it points the other way. `toolbox` takes tools away from
    # containers; this one gives two back, because the shell config never stopped
    # referring to them. .bash_env exports EDITOR=nvim and sources forgit's plugin
    # with no gate at all, and .bash_aliases builds `gg` on top of forgit, so a
    # container had a dangling EDITOR and six dead git aliases that the README
    # cheatsheet still advertised. That is the floor's own bar ("the config wires
    # it in unconditionally") — forgit therefore moved into the floor outright,
    # and neovim, which costs 1.1GB because nvim-treesitter needs a C/C++
    # compiler to build parsers, sits here instead so it lands only where it is
    # actually driven.
    #
    # On for `personal` and `container` only, which is deliberately NOT the same
    # set as `toolbox`. `shared` and `robot` had no nvim before this flag existed
    # (it was gated on `heavy`), they have vim from the toolbox, and neither wants
    # a compiler toolchain on a lab PC or an appliance — so they keep the status
    # quo. `kinisi` is not addressable from here either way: `pixi` is off for it,
    # so the manifest this flag gates is never written there at all; it installs
    # from container/pixi-global.toml instead.
    editor    = {{ or $personal (eq $profile "container") }}   # neovim + its config tree
    # Ownership flags — which parts of $HOME chezmoi is allowed to write. False where
    # something else owns the path: a host bind-mount, the image, or the container runtime.
    pixi      = {{ ne $profile "kinisi" }}                    # ~/.pixi/manifests/pixi-global.toml
    xdg       = {{ ne $profile "kinisi" }}                    # ~/.config, ~/.cache, ~/.local/share
    gitconfig = {{ not $containerish }}                       # ~/.gitconfig
{{/* `claudecfg` is the one ownership flag that is not read off the profile name, and
     it is the "observed fact" this file's header asks for. Claude's config is
     ordinary dotfiles content and a container needs it exactly as much as a host
     does; what a container must not do is write it back out through somebody else's
     bind mount. Those are different questions, and the profile name only ever
     answered the first one, wrongly -- see "Who owns ~/.claude" in the README.

     The empty fall-through keeps the old profile-based answer, and is reachable only
     by a hand-run `chezmoi init` that skipped install.sh. Nothing has observed the
     mount table on that path, so it keeps the guess it always made: containers do
     not write ~/.claude. Every scripted path goes through install.sh, DevPod's
     dotfiles step included, and replaces the guess with the fact in both
     directions.

     Kept as a template comment rather than a `#` one because this is generated
     per machine and the rationale does not belong in every copy of it. */}}
    # Observed by install.sh, not derived from the profile: see the README.
    claudecfg = {{ if eq $claudeMount "foreign" }}false{{ else if eq $claudeMount "local" }}true{{ else }}{{ not $containerish }}{{ end }}                       # ~/.claude

[git]
    # Disable autoCommit to avoid prompts in non-interactive environments
    autoCommit = false

[template]
    options = ["missingkey=error"]
